{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-ini-itu",
  "connector": "ini / itu",
  "gloss": "this and that",
  "title": "ini and itu — this and that",
  "pattern": "Ini … (This is …) · … ini (this …)",
  "explanation": "Ini means 'this' and itu 'that', and where you put them changes the meaning. Before a noun they make a sentence: Ini kopi is 'This is coffee', and Itu rumah saya is 'That's my house'. After a noun they point: kopi ini is 'this coffee', rumah itu 'that house'. They also cover 'these' and 'those', because the noun doesn't change: sepatu ini can be 'these shoes'.",
  "natural": "Ini and itu do a lot of pointing in shops and warungs: Ini apa? (What's this?), Yang itu, Bu (That one, please). After a noun, itu often means no more than 'the', for something you've both already mentioned: Hotel itu mahal? (Is the hotel expensive?)",
  "stiff": "Mixing up the two positions changes the sentence: Ini buku baru is 'This is a new book', while Buku ini baru is 'This book is new'. Ini and itu go after the whole description: rumah besar itu is 'that big house', but rumah itu besar is a sentence, 'That house is big'.",
